@* Building the program.
The build is described for CMake, which generates a Visual Studio solution. Three
libraries are downloaded by CMake itself the first time the project is configured, each
pinned to a precise version so that the build is reproducible: Dear ImGui (the
\emph{docking} branch, which provides the dockable panels), \texttt{stb\_image} and
\texttt{nlohmann/json}.

<<file:CMakeLists.txt>>=
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)
project(TextureExplorer LANGUAGES CXX)

set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)

<<Fetch the libraries>>

<<Build Dear ImGui as a static library>>

<<Build the program>>

@
<<Fetch the libraries>>=
include(FetchContent)
FetchContent_Declare(imgui
    GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/ocornut/imgui.git
    GIT_TAG        v1.91.9b-docking
    GIT_SHALLOW    TRUE)
FetchContent_Declare(stb
    GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/nothings/stb.git
    GIT_TAG        2c980bb59875b0d32144a71867fbdebb2f77cd20)
FetchContent_Declare(json
    URL https://github.com/nlohmann/json/releases/download/v3.11.3/json.tar.xz)
FetchContent_MakeAvailable(imgui stb json)

@ Dear ImGui does not come with a build description of its own; it expects its few
source files to be compiled with the program, together with the two ``back ends'' that
connect it to Win32 and to Direct3D~11.

<<Build Dear ImGui as a static library>>=
add_library(imgui STATIC
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/imgui.cpp
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/imgui_draw.cpp
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/imgui_tables.cpp
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/imgui_widgets.cpp
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/imgui_demo.cpp
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/backends/imgui_impl_win32.cpp
    ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/backends/imgui_impl_dx11.cpp)
target_include_directories(imgui PUBLIC ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR} ${imgui_SOURCE_DIR}/backends)

@ The program is a |WIN32| executable (it has no console). The definition |TEXEX_ROOT|
records the source folder for |FindRoot| in <<Private helpers of the application>>;
|NOMINMAX| keeps \texttt{windows.h} from defining macros called |min| and |max|, which would
break |std::min| and |std::max|. The compiler is asked for warning level 4 and strict
conformance; the program compiles without a single warning.

<<Build the program>>=
add_executable(TextureExplorer WIN32
    src/main.cpp
    src/App.cpp
    src/Renderer.cpp
    src/Material.cpp
    src/Mesh.cpp
    src/Inspector.cpp)
target_include_directories(TextureExplorer PRIVATE src ${stb_SOURCE_DIR})
target_link_libraries(TextureExplorer PRIVATE imgui nlohmann_json::nlohmann_json
    d3d11 dxgi d3dcompiler shell32)
target_compile_definitions(TextureExplorer PRIVATE
    TEXEX_ROOT="${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}" UNICODE _UNICODE NOMINMAX WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN)
if(MSVC)
    target_compile_options(TextureExplorer PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- /utf-8)
    set_property(DIRECTORY PROPERTY VS_STARTUP_PROJECT TextureExplorer)
endif()

@* Exercises.
A literate program invites modification. Here are some changes that a student can make,
in increasing order of difficulty, each of which illuminates one of the ideas above.

\begin{enumerate}
\item In <<Patches of the sphere>>, remove the minus sign of the $z$ coordinate (and of the
derivatives). Look at the texture, and then at the inspector's $\vect T$ and $\vect B$:
what happened to the rule $\vect T\times\vect B=-\vect N$, and to the sign $w$?
\item In <<The normal of a height field, on the GPU>> and
<<The normal of a height field>>, replace the central differences by forward
differences, $h(x+1)-h(x)$. Compare the probe's normal before and after on a sharp edge of
a brick.
\item Make the pyramid repeat the whole texture on each face. What must change in
<<Patches of the pyramid>>, and why can the inspector no longer find a unique point?
\item Add a view mode that shows the \emph{difference} between the normal computed from
the bump map and the decoded normal map of the material.
\item Recompute the normal in the vertex shader from the displacement map, so that
displacement alone produces correct shading. (Hint: the same central differences, on the
displacement map, at the level |dispLod|.)
\item Replace the vertex displacement by hardware tessellation with hull and domain
shaders, so that the mesh can be coarse far from the camera.
\end{enumerate}
